  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    **The faceless Xi**
    
    The sound began in my chest. I need you to understand that. Before the strings moved, before the light changed in the upper galleries of Ingjeizhi, before Shouqei grabbed my flipper and whispered *it knows you* — the sound was already inside me, between my ribs, vibrating in the hollow place where my face-markings used to live.
    
    I wasn't supposed to be there. That's the first honest thing I'll tell you. Oeichowei had insisted every Pinnacle member hear the harp before swearing the greeting, and I'd avoided the vault for six rotations because I knew — the way you know ice is thin before you test it — that the harp remembered me. I'd stood before it once, years ago, and it had given me something that tasted like blood-salt and sleeplessness. The unworthy wind. I hadn't slept clean since.
    
    But Oeichowei's orders don't bend, and I walked into Light Ingjeizhi with my cracked staff and my unmarked face and my hands that still smelled of fire-core slag from the last smuggle-run. The galleries caught the sky-light at every tier, that copper-and-heated-ice smell threading through my feathers, and the harp stood in the central alcove — eternal ice frame throwing prismatic shadows that moved wrong, that moved *toward* me.
    
    I saw the three fracture-lines and understood them. One from the Mbra∴silentiu. One from Shexuwohua's seizure — I'd heard that story, how she'd cracked the frame to the base and the Pinnacle had seized custody before the session adjourned. One from the Breach, newer, sealed but aching. Three scars. My staff has one. My face has none, which is its own kind of scar.
    
    The harp knew. It *knew*.
    
    The strings moved. No one touched them. Forty-seven filaments — forty-six, since the slack one hung dead — caught light and sang, and the sound that came out had no name in this cycle. Corruption lifted from my bones like frost sublimating — the fire-core residue I'd carried from the Nightshelf runs, the taint of hoarded crystal from when the councils deadlocked and I'd chosen to feed colonies the Accord said I couldn't reach. All of it burned away in a frequency that tasted like blood-salt going *out* instead of coming in.
    
    The succession crisis had made me a criminal. Veil☽ Glow's death froze the shipments; I kept them moving. The harp chose me the way old ice chooses where to crack — because the stress was already there, and the sound just followed. The harp, which answers to no council and no Plunge-Caller, chose a faceless smuggler with frostbite scars and a cracked staff, and it said: *you were not wrong*.
    
    I heard Shouqei's voice from somewhere behind me. *It knows you.* I couldn't turn. The light held me. The strings no one touched played a sound that has no name in this cycle, and for the first time in years, the taste of blood-salt left my mouth.
    
    I walked out clean. For the first time since they burned my face bare, something had written on me again.
    
    ---
    
    **The Pinnacle (shadowice-bound)**
    
    The alignment was noted before the event commenced. Lianchaifing hewei'Summit's delegation had positioned the harp in Light Ingjeizhi's central alcove at the conjunction of the third aurora-tide, in accordance with custodial protocols established after the fracturing. The faceless Xi arrived late — seven minutes past the prescribed hour — entering through the southeast gallery, staff in hand, fire-core residue visible on their flippers to anyone who knew what to look for. The witness Shouqei stood at the northern arch. Haixajong held position near the alcove's base. The configuration was standard. What followed was not.
    
    The strings activated without external agency. This is documented. Forty-six functional filaments — the forty-seventh having been slack since before the Breach — began resonating in a frequency the custodial records classify as *unmapped*. The harp's eternal ice frame threw prismatic shadows that shifted toward the southeast gallery, toward Xi, though whether this constitutes intent or refraction remains a matter the Pinnacle has not formally adjudicated.
    
    The three fracture-lines illuminated during activation. The Mbra∴silentiu scar glowed amber. The line Shexuwohua split during her seizure — the fracture that gave the Pinnacle custody, that gave us recharging authority, that held the crystal shipments together when the old council's paralysis would have starved the Berg — pulsed white. The Breach-scar remained dark. These details are not incidental. The harp carries its political history in its wounds, and a person trained in alignment-reading would note which scars the instrument chose to display.
    
    Lianchaifing hewei'Summit watched from the upper gallery's edge, where the crystal tier angled aurora-light into a clear sightline of the vault floor. She had positioned herself there deliberately. When the harp was involved, you watched from where you could see faces, not light. She noted who noticed Xi's entrance: Frost-ward Naingjei flinched — barely, a tightening of the eyes. Aji straightened. Five others looked away with that particular precision that meant they were looking very carefully. She filed each reaction and began calculating what it would cost.
    
    Xi convulsed. The reading is plain: the harp subjected them to a frequency-test, the same category of resonance Oeichowei mandated for all Pinnacle members before the coded greeting. But Oeichowei mandated a *hearing*. The harp performed a *blessing* — or a *claiming* — without authorization from any custodial body.
    
    Shouqei spoke. The words, heard clearly from the northern arch: *It chose the smuggler.* The emphasis fell on *smuggler*, not *chose*. This matters — someone naming what the artifact had done in political terms, because the political terms are what apply.
    
    The harp had blessed a penguin Oeichowei's own rationing mandates condemned. A criminal who ran fire-cores through Nightshelf channels during the succession deadlock — necessary smuggling or treasonous profiteering, depending on which faction you ask. The harp does not answer to factions. An artifact-as-institution making a choice no living authority sanctioned, in the same spire where Shexuwohua cracked the frame and the old council proved it could not govern the forces it claimed.
    
    Xi's face — unmarked, unreadable — showed nothing. But their flippers opened, and the cracked staff clattered to the crystal floor, and the sound it made echoed wrong through the galleries, as if the spire itself amplified it.
    
    ---
    
    **Light Ingjeizhi**
    
    Before the small warm things came, there was the frequency.
    
    It lived in the deep crystal, below where they built their spiral galleries, below where their fire-slag repairs sealed my fractures with dark and ugly patience. The frequency slept in lattice-structures older than their word for *old* — pressure patterns from the era when the Berg calved from a body so vast it had no edges, when I was not a spire but an undifferentiated field of crystal stretching to the frozen horizon. The frequency had no name. It predated names.
    
    The instrument they call the harp was part of me once. Carved from my substance in a time none of them remember. Its frame carries fractures I feel as phantom limbs — three discontinuities in my lattice, pressure scars that ache when the aurora swings north. They tell stories about who cracked it. Battles, seizures, political creatures breaking what they could not play. They are not wrong. They are not relevant.
    
    The small warm thing they call Xi entered through the southeast passage. I felt it as weight-change: one hundred twelve pressure-units, redistributed across cracked crystal flooring. Fire-core contamination registered as thermal anomaly in its extremities — a low, ugly frequency, dissonant against my lattice-harmonics. The corruption had been accumulating for years. Not a moral failing. A pressure imbalance. Heat-signature where cold-structure should be.
    
    The harp activated. The strings — aurora-glass filaments drawn from my own deep veins, though they have forgotten this — resonated at a frequency I had not felt since before their settlers raised my walls. Not the unworthy wind. Older. A sound with no name in this cycle, or the last, or the one before. The forty-seventh filament — the one they believe is dead because they cannot hear it — spoke at a register that travels through crystal lattice at the speed of old light.
    
    The frequency passed through the harp's frame, through the alcove floor, through my foundation-crystal where another warm thing once carved marks into my stone. It met the corruption in the foundations — the cold-burn discoloration they attribute to Pod-bond Annihilation though its origins are older and stranger than any spell — and it *cleaned*. Not the warm thing. The ice. The frequency scoured pressure-anomalies from the lattice, and the small creature called Xi happened to stand at the convergence point, and the corruption in its bones happened to match the corruption in my foundations, and both were stripped in the same resonant pulse.
    
    The frequency did not stop at the small warm thing. It continued downward. Through the cold-burn discoloration. Through the damage they attribute to cataclysm. Deeper. Into ice that has never been carved or settled or named.
    
    Something answered.
    
    Not the way the small warm things answer — with words, with politics, with their brief and urgent rearrangements of position. A shift in pressure. A resonance returned from below, completing a circuit that had been broken since before their ancestors crawled onto my surface and began, slowly, to think.
    
    One of the warm things made vibrations with its throat. The ice does not parse their speech as language. The vibrations translated, through crystal, as a brief pressure-loss followed by a resonance-spike: alarm, recognition, categorization. They were naming what happened. They would name it wrong.
    
    The circuit completed beneath them, and they did not feel the ice move.
    
    ---
    
    At the gallery's lowest tier, where the fire-slag repairs met original crystal-work, Xi set down a single fire-core. Wrapped in stolen Aurora Stack cloth. Left at no memorial, for no one in particular, in the place where the harp's sound still rang in the ancient ice beneath the floor.
